\begin{abstract}
For an integral point $P=(x,y)$ on the Mordell curve $E_N : y^2 = x^3 + N$ we study the primes occurring in the denominator of the $x$-coordinate of the multiple $nP$, and we complete the analysis of the quadrupling layer $n=4$. Starting from the identity $(x^4-8Nx)^3 + 64N(x^3+N)^3 = (x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2)^2$, valid over any commutative ring, we derive the quadrupling formula and prove a non\-cancellation theorem whose exceptional constants are $3^8$ and $2^63^2$; consequently, for every prime $\ell\ge 5$ with $\ell\nmid N$ one has $\ell \mid \den x(4P)$ if and only if $\ell \mid (x^3+N)(x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2)$. Counting solutions modulo $\ell$, we prove that the layer-$4$ total equals $3\ell-2$ when $3$ is a quadratic residue modulo $\ell$ and $\ell$ otherwise, and deduce that no linear law with bounded error holds; this refutes the extrapolation, natural from the layer-$2$ and layer-$3$ totals $\ell$ and $2\ell-1$, that the layer-$n$ total is the number of irreducible factors of the $n$-th division polynomial times $\ell$, up to a bounded error. We also show that layer $4$ activates exactly the residue classes of $N$ that layer $2$ activates, via a hidden cube root supplied by $\bigl(\tfrac{-1-\sqrt3}{2}\bigr)^3 = -\tfrac{5+3\sqrt3}{4}$. Finally, for every bound $B$ and every semiprime $N=pq$ with $p,q>B$ there is a prime $M>N$ for which the layer-$2$, layer-$3$ and layer-$4$ criteria at all primes $\ell\le B$ agree with those of $N$: the small-prime denominator profile of the first four layers carries no information about the factorisation of $N$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Mordell curve, elliptic divisibility, division polynomial, denominator prime, quadratic reciprocity, Chebotarev density, integer factorisation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}

Let $N$ be a nonzero integer and let
\[ E_N : y^2 = x^3 + N \]
be the associated Mordell curve, an elliptic curve over $\QQ$ of discriminant $-432N^2$ and $j$-invariant $0$. Its rational points form a finitely generated abelian group under the chord\-and\-tangent law. An integral point $P=(x,y)\in E_N(\ZZ)$ has, in general, non-integral multiples: writing $x(nP)=a_n/b_n$ in lowest terms, the denominators $b_n$ grow rapidly and factor into primes not visible in $N$, $x$ or $y$.

A concrete instance, used throughout as a running example, is $N=55$, $P=(9,28)$ (indeed $28^2=784=9^3+55$). One computes
\[ x(2P)=\frac{2601}{3136}=\frac{3^2\cdot17^2}{2^6\cdot7^2},\qquad
   x(4P)=\frac{-35249882584054239}{2^8\cdot7^2\cdot827^2\cdot1583^2}. \]
The primes $7$, $827$, $1583$ have been \emph{manufactured} by iterating the group law.

\subsection{Reduction-theoretic meaning}

For a prime $\ell\nmid 6N$ the curve $E_N$ has good reduction at $\ell$, and reduction modulo $\ell$ is a group homomorphism $E_N(\QQ)\to E_N(\FF_\ell)$ whose kernel consists of the points with non-integral coordinates at $\ell$. Hence
\[ \ell\mid\den x(nP)\iff \text{the reduction } \bar P\in E_N(\FF_\ell) \text{ satisfies } n\bar P=O. \]
Denominator primes at layer $n$ are exactly the primes at which $P$ becomes $n$-torsion. Since $n$-torsion is cut out by the $n$-th division polynomial $\psi_n$, the question becomes one about polynomial congruences. For the Mordell family,
\[ \psi_2 = 2y,\qquad \psi_3 = 3x^4+12Nx=3x(x^3+4N),\qquad \psi_4=4y\,(x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2). \]

\subsection{Background and contribution}

Two earlier layers are settled. At \emph{layer $2$}, for an integral point with $y\ne0$ and every prime $\ell\ge5$ with $\ell\nmid N$, one has $\ell\mid\den x(2P)\iff\ell\mid x^3+N$; writing $V_2(c)=\{t\in\FF_\ell: t^3+c=0\}$, the total $\sum_c\#V_2(c)$ equals $\ell$ exactly, $V_2(c)\ne\emptyset$ iff $-c$ is a cube modulo $\ell$, and for $\ell\equiv1\pmod3$ exactly $2(\ell-1)/3$ residues are \emph{blind}, the active density being $(\ell+2)/(3\ell)$. At \emph{layer $3$}, $\ell\mid\den x(3P)\iff\ell\mid 3x(x^3+4N)$, the exceptional numerator values on the two branches being $64N^3$ and $-1728N^3$; the layer-$3$ total is $2\ell-1$, and layer $3$ is active over every residue class of $N$ because of the free root $x\equiv0$. Finally, layers $2$ and $3$ are already known to be subject to an information barrier below any fixed bound.

Extrapolating from the totals $\ell$ and $2\ell-1$, it is natural to conjecture that the layer-$n$ total is $k_n\ell+O(1)$ with $k_n$ the number of $\QQ(N)$-irreducible factors of $\psi_n$; and, from the exceptional constants $9$, $64$, $1728$, that at every layer the exceptional values of the numerator on the locus $\psi_n=0$ are $\{2,3\}$-units times powers of $N$. This paper settles layer $4$, the first layer at which $\psi_n$ acquires a factor which is not of Kummer type $T^k+cN^j$. The second prediction is \emph{confirmed}; the first is \emph{refuted} and replaced by a reciprocity-governed statement.

\section{Setup and the layer-4 polynomials}

Throughout, $N$ is a nonzero integer, $\ell$ a prime with $\ell\ge5$, and $P=(x,y)$ an integral point of $E_N$. For $\alpha\in\QQ$ we write $\den\alpha$ for the positive denominator of $\alpha$ in lowest terms.

\begin{definition}\label{def:poly}
Over $\ZZ[N,x]$ set
\[ S(N,x):=x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2,\qquad \Psi_4(N,x):=(x^3+N)\,S(N,x), \]
\[ \varphi_4(N,x):=(x^4-8Nx)\bigl((x^4-8Nx)^3-512N(x^3+N)^3\bigr),\qquad \Delta_4(N,x):=16\,(x^3+N)\,S(N,x)^2 . \]
\end{definition}

Expanding, $\Psi_4(N,x)=x^9+21Nx^6+12N^2x^3-8N^3$.

\begin{lemma}[key identity]\label{lem:key}
In any commutative ring,
\[ (x^4-8Nx)^3+64N\,(x^3+N)^3=\bigl(x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2\bigr)^2 . \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both sides expand to $x^{12}+40Nx^9+384N^2x^6-320N^3x^3+64N^4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:square}
If $x^3+N\ne0$ and $X:=(x^4-8Nx)/\bigl(4(x^3+N)\bigr)$, then
\[ X^3+N=\frac{S(N,x)^2}{64\,(x^3+N)^3}. \]
\end{corollary}

In words: the value of the defining cubic at the doubled point is a perfect square divided by the cube of the doubling denominator. Equivalently $y(2P)=\pm S(N,x)/\bigl(8(x^3+N)^2\bigr)$, which is precisely why $\psi_4=4y\,S(N,x)$: the sextic $S$ \emph{is} the square root handed to us by Lemma~\ref{lem:key}.

\begin{lemma}[transversality]\label{lem:trans}
If $x^3+N=0$ and $S(N,x)=0$ then $27N^2=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From $x^3=-N$ we get $x^6=N^2$, so $S=N^2-20N^2-8N^2=-27N^2$.
\end{proof}

Thus over any field of characteristic at least $5$ the branches $x^3+N=0$ and $S=0$ are disjoint unless $N=0$. The constant $27=3^3$ is again a discriminant constant of the family.

\section{The quadrupling layer}

\begin{lemma}[doubling]\label{lem:double}
Let $P=(x,y)$ be a nonsingular rational point of $E_N$ with $y\ne0$. Then
\[ x(2P)=\frac{x^4-8Nx}{4(x^3+N)}=\frac{x^4-8Nx}{4y^2}. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The tangent slope is $\lambda=3x^2/(2y)$ and $x(2P)=\lambda^2-2x=(9x^4-8xy^2)/(4y^2)$; substituting $y^2=x^3+N$ gives $(x^4-8Nx)/(4y^2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[quadrupling formula]\label{thm:quad}
Let $P=(x,y)$ be a nonsingular rational point of $E_N$ with $y\ne0$ and $S(N,x)\ne0$ (equivalently, $P$ is neither $2$- nor $4$-torsion). Then
\[ x(4P)=\frac{(x^4-8Nx)\bigl((x^4-8Nx)^3-512N(x^3+N)^3\bigr)}{16\,(x^3+N)\,\bigl(x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2\bigr)^2}=\frac{\varphi_4(N,x)}{\Delta_4(N,x)} . \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $2P=(X,Y)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:double}, $X=(x^4-8Nx)/(4(x^3+N))$, and by Corollary~\ref{cor:square}, $Y^2=X^3+N=S^2/\bigl(64(x^3+N)^3\bigr)$, which is nonzero exactly under the hypothesis $S\ne0$. Hence $2P$ is again an affine non-$2$-torsion point and Lemma~\ref{lem:double} applies to it:
\[ x(4P)=\frac{X^4-8NX}{4(X^3+N)}=\frac{X(X^3-8N)}{4(X^3+N)} . \]
Substituting the two displayed expressions for $X$ and $X^3+N$ and clearing denominators yields the stated rational function.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[non-cancellation]\label{thm:noncanc}
Let $\ell\ge5$ be a prime with $\ell\nmid N$ and suppose $\ell\mid\Psi_4(N,x)$. Then $\ell\nmid\varphi_4(N,x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Work in $\FF_\ell$, writing $X$ for $x\bmod\ell$ and $M\ne0$ for $N\bmod\ell$. The hypothesis says $(X^3+M)S(M,X)=0$.

\emph{Branch A: $X^3=-M$.} Then $X\ne0$ and $X^4-8MX=-MX-8MX=-9MX$, while $X^3+M=0$, so the second factor of $\varphi_4$ equals $(-9MX)^3=-729M^3X^3=729M^4$. Hence $\varphi_4=(-9MX)\cdot729M^4=-3^8M^5X\ne0$, using $\ell\ge5$.

\emph{Branch B: $S(M,X)=0$.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:trans} and $M\ne0$ we get $X^3+M\ne0$. Lemma~\ref{lem:key} gives $A^3=-64M(X^3+M)^3\ne0$ for $A:=X^4-8MX$, so $A\ne0$, and
\[ \varphi_4=A\bigl(A^3-512M(X^3+M)^3\bigr)=-\,(64+512)\,M\,A\,(X^3+M)^3=-2^63^2\,M\,A\,(X^3+M)^3\ne0 . \qedhere \]
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The exceptional constants are $3^8=6561$ and $2^63^2=576$; they join the layer-$2$ constant $9=3^2$ and the layer-$3$ constants $64=2^6$ and $1728=2^63^3$. All are $\{2,3\}$-units, and $2$, $3$ are exactly the primes dividing the discriminant $-432N^2=-2^43^3N^2$ of the Mordell family. This confirms, at the first genuinely non-Kummer layer, that $\ell\ge5$ and $\ell\nmid N$ are the only hypotheses needed.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[layer-4 criterion]\label{thm:crit}
Let $P=(x,y)$ be an integral point of $E_N$ with $y\ne0$ and $S(N,x)\ne0$, and let $\ell\ge5$ be a prime with $\ell\nmid N$. Then
\[ \ell\mid\den x(4P)\iff \ell\mid\Psi_4(N,x)=(x^3+N)\bigl(x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:quad}, $x(4P)=\varphi_4/\Delta_4$ with $\Delta_4=16(x^3+N)S^2$. If $\ell\mid\Psi_4$ then $\ell$ divides $\Delta_4$ but, by Theorem~\ref{thm:noncanc}, not $\varphi_4$, so $\ell$ survives to the reduced denominator. Conversely the reduced denominator divides $\Delta_4$, and $\ell\nmid16$ since $\ell\ge5$, so $\ell$ divides $(x^3+N)$ or $S^2$, hence divides $\Psi_4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[running example]\label{prop:55}
For $N=55$, $P=(9,28)$,
\[ \Psi_4(55,9)=(9^3+55)\bigl(9^6+20\cdot55\cdot9^3-8\cdot55^2\bigr)=784\cdot1309141=2^4\cdot7^2\cdot827\cdot1583 . \]
Hence each of $7$, $827$, $1583$ divides $\den x(4P)$; in fact $\den x(4P)=2^8\cdot7^2\cdot827^2\cdot1583^2$.
\end{proposition}

At layer $2$ only $7$ occurs, and at layer $3$ only $13$ and $73$; layer $4$ contributes two new large primes from a single polynomial evaluation.

\section{Class counts: a reciprocity dichotomy}

Fix a prime $\ell\ge5$ and work in $\FF_\ell$.

\begin{definition}
For $c\in\FF_\ell$ (thought of as $N\bmod\ell$) put
\[ V_2(c):=\{t: t^3+c=0\},\quad W_4(c):=\{t: t^6+20ct^3-8c^2=0\},\quad V_4(c):=V_2(c)\cup W_4(c), \]
and set $T_4(\ell):=\sum_{c\in\FF_\ell}\#V_4(c)$, the \emph{layer-$4$ total}; analogously $T_2,T_3$.
\end{definition}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:crit}, membership $x\bmod\ell\in V_4(N\bmod\ell)$ is exactly the layer-$4$ divisibility criterion.

\begin{lemma}[the Kummer layers]\label{lem:kummer}
$T_2(\ell)=\ell$ and $T_3(\ell)=2\ell-1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transpose the count: $T_2(\ell)=\sum_t\#\{c:t^3+c=0\}$, and each $t$ admits exactly one $c$, namely $c=-t^3$. For layer $3$ the locus is the union of $\{t=0\}$ (all $\ell$ values of $c$) and $\{t^3=-4c\}$ (one $c$ per $t$), overlapping only at $(0,0)$.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is that a factor \emph{linear in $N$} has singleton fibres, yielding exactly $\ell$ per irreducible factor. Since $\Psi_4=(T^3+N)\cdot S$ with $S$ irreducible over $\QQ(N)$, the factor-counting heuristic predicts $T_4(\ell)=2\ell+O(1)$.

\begin{definition}
For $t\in\FF_\ell$ let $W^{\mathrm{fib}}(t):=\{c\in\FF_\ell : t^6+20ct^3-8c^2=0\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[completing the square]\label{lem:cs}
For $\ell\ge5$ and $t,c\in\FF_\ell$: $\;t^6+20ct^3-8c^2=0\iff(4c-5t^3)^2=27\,(t^3)^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$(4c-5t^3)^2-27t^6=-2\,(t^6+20ct^3-8c^2)$ and $2$ is invertible.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[fibre sizes]\label{lem:fib}
Let $\ell\ge5$. Then $\#W^{\mathrm{fib}}(0)=1$; and for $t\ne0$, $\#W^{\mathrm{fib}}(t)=2$ if $3$ is a square modulo $\ell$, and $\#W^{\mathrm{fib}}(t)=0$ otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At $t=0$ the equation is $-8c^2=0$ with $8$ invertible. For $t\ne0$, Lemma~\ref{lem:cs} says $c$ is a solution iff $4c-5t^3$ is a square root of $27t^6$; since $t^6$ is a square and $27=3^3$, this is solvable iff $3$ is a square. If $3=r^2$, put $s:=3r$, so $s^2=27$ and the solutions are $c_{1,2}=(5t^3\pm st^3)/4$, distinct since $2,s,t\ne0$. If some $c$ existed with $3$ a non-square, then $\bigl((4c-5t^3)/(3t^3)\bigr)^2=3$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[the new locus]\label{thm:W4}
For $\ell\ge5$,
\[ \sum_{c\in\FF_\ell}\#W_4(c)=\sum_{t\in\FF_\ell}\#W^{\mathrm{fib}}(t)=\begin{cases} 2\ell-1, & 3 \text{ a square mod }\ell,\\ 1,&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases} \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first equality is Fubini for the incidence set $\{(c,t):S(c,t)=0\}$; the second is Lemma~\ref{lem:fib}, splitting off $t=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[overlap]\label{lem:overlap}
For $\ell\ge5$, $\sum_c\#\bigl(V_2(c)\cap W_4(c)\bigr)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $t^3=-c$ and $S(c,t)=0$ then $c^2-20c^2-8c^2=-27c^2=0$, so $c=0$ and $t=0$; conversely $(0,0)$ lies in both.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[layer-4 total]\label{thm:total}
For every prime $\ell\ge5$,
\[ T_4(\ell)=\begin{cases} 3\ell-2, & 3 \text{ a square mod }\ell,\\ \ell, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases} \]
In particular $T_4(7)=7$, $T_4(13)=37$, $T_4(19)=19$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Classwise inclusion--exclusion gives $\#V_4(c)+\#(V_2(c)\cap W_4(c))=\#V_2(c)+\#W_4(c)$. Sum over $c$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:kummer}, Theorem~\ref{thm:W4} and Lemma~\ref{lem:overlap}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[the two regimes]\label{prop:recip}
For a prime $\ell\ge5$: $3$ is a square modulo $\ell$ iff $\ell\equiv\pm1\pmod{12}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Quadratic reciprocity for the odd primes $3$ and $\ell$ gives $\bigl(\tfrac3\ell\bigr)\bigl(\tfrac\ell3\bigr)=(-1)^{(\ell-1)/2}$; combining the evaluation of $\bigl(\tfrac\ell3\bigr)$ (which is $+1$ iff $\ell\equiv1\bmod3$, $2$ being a non-square modulo $3$) with the sign determined by $\ell\bmod4$ yields the four classes modulo $12$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[refutation of linear growth]\label{thm:refute}
There is no pair $(k,C)\in\NN^2$ with $k\ell-C\le T_4(\ell)\le k\ell+C$ for all primes $\ell\ge5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Dirichlet's theorem choose a prime $\ell_2\equiv5\pmod{12}$ with $\ell_2>C+10$. Then $T_4(\ell_2)=\ell_2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:total} and Proposition~\ref{prop:recip}, and the two inequalities force $k=1$ (for $k\ge2$ we would need $2\ell_2\le\ell_2+C$; for $k=0$, $\ell_2\le C$). Now choose a prime $\ell_1\equiv1\pmod{12}$ with $\ell_1>C+10$: then $T_4(\ell_1)=3\ell_1-2\le\ell_1+C$ gives $2\ell_1\le C+2$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{discussion}\label{disc:cheb}
The failure is structural. Transposing the count reduces the layer-$n$ total to a sum of fibre sizes $\#\{c: f(t,c)=0\}$ over the irreducible factors $f$ of $\psi_n$. A factor linear in $N$ has fibre size $1$ and contributes $\ell$; a factor of higher degree in $N$ contributes its average number of roots, which by Chebotarev's density theorem is governed by the Frobenius class of $\ell$ in the splitting field of $f$ over $\QQ(N)$. At layer $4$ that field is $\QQ(\sqrt3)$, and the resulting class function takes the two values $3$ and $1$. The discredited prediction $2\ell$ is exactly the mean of $3\ell-2$ and $\ell$, which is why it looked plausible. The corrected general expectation is $T_n(\ell)=f_n(\Frob_\ell)\cdot\ell+O_n(1)$ for a class function $f_n$ attached to $\psi_n$, with $f_2\equiv1$, $f_3\equiv2$ and $f_4\in\{1,3\}$.
\end{discussion}

\section{Layer 4 activates no new residues}

Call a residue $c=N\bmod\ell$ \emph{active at layer $n$} if the layer-$n$ locus over $c$ is nonempty. Layer $2$ is active iff $-c$ is a cube modulo $\ell$, so for $\ell\equiv1\pmod3$ exactly $2(\ell-1)/3$ residues are blind; layer $3$ is active everywhere thanks to the $N$-free root $t\equiv0$. Layer $4$ has strictly more roots on average, but sees nothing new.

\begin{theorem}[the hidden cube]\label{thm:hidden}
Let $\ell\ge5$ and $c\in\FF_\ell$. If $W_4(c)\ne\emptyset$ then $V_2(c)\ne\emptyset$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $t\in W_4(c)$. If $t=0$ then $8c^2=0$, so $c=0$ and $0\in V_2(0)$. If $t\ne0$, set $g:=(4c-5t^3)/(3t^3)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:cs}, $g^2=27t^6/(9t^6)=3$, and $4c=t^3(5+3g)$ by construction. In $\ZZ[g]/(g^2-3)$,
\[ \left(\frac{-1-g}{2}\right)^{3}=\frac{-(1+3g+3g^2+g^3)}{8}=\frac{-(1+3g+9+3g)}{8}=-\frac{5+3g}{4}, \]
so $\bigl(\tfrac{-1-g}{2}t\bigr)^3=-\tfrac{(5+3g)t^3}{4}=-c$, i.e. $\tfrac{-1-g}{2}t\in V_2(c)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every prime $\ell\ge5$ and every $c$: $V_4(c)\ne\emptyset\iff V_2(c)\ne\emptyset$. Consequently, if $-N$ is not a cube modulo $\ell$ then $\ell\nmid\den x(4P)$ for every integral point $P=(x,y)$ of $E_N$ with $y\ne0$ and $S(N,x)\ne0$. Moreover, if $3$ is not a square modulo $\ell$ then $V_4(c)=V_2(c)$ for all $c\ne0$.
\end{corollary}

Activity along the tower is therefore \emph{not} monotone: layer $2$ is partially blind, layer $3$ is universally active, and layer $4$ relapses to layer $2$'s field of vision while possibly tripling the number of producing pairs. The distinguishing feature is the presence of an $N$-free root of $\psi_n$, which $\psi_3$ has and $\psi_2,\psi_4$ do not.

\section{The information barrier through layer 4}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:polydiff}
$\Psi_4(N,x)-\Psi_4(M,x)=(N-M)\bigl(21x^6+12x^3(N+M)-8(N^2+NM+M^2)\bigr)$. In particular, if $\ell\mid N-M$ then $\ell\mid\Psi_4(N,x)\iff\ell\mid\Psi_4(M,x)$ for every integer $x$; the same holds for $x^3+N$ and for $3x^4+12Nx$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[barrier]\label{thm:barrier}
Let $B\in\NN$ and let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ primes exceeding $B$. Then there exists a prime $M>N$ such that for every prime $\ell\le B$ and every integer $x$,
\[ \ell\mid x^3+N\iff\ell\mid x^3+M,\qquad \ell\mid 3x^4+12Nx\iff\ell\mid3x^4+12Mx,\qquad \ell\mid\Psi_4(N,x)\iff\ell\mid\Psi_4(M,x). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $p,q>B$, the integer $N=pq$ is coprime to $B!$, so by Dirichlet's theorem the class of $N$ modulo $B!$ contains infinitely many primes; pick one with $M>N$. Any prime $\ell\le B$ divides $B!$, hence $\ell\mid M-N$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:polydiff} applies at all three layers.
\end{proof}

\begin{discussion}
The barrier says that the entire small-prime denominator profile of the first four layers is a function of $N\bmod B!$ alone. Since a prime $M$ shares that residue, no statistic computed from these data distinguishes a semiprime from a prime, let alone reveals $p$ or $q$. This is an information-theoretic statement, not a hardness assumption. It also isolates what a hypothetical elliptic-denominator factoring method would have to use: primes $\ell$ comparable in size to $p,q$; or the $\ell$-adic \emph{valuations} rather than mere occurrence; or the specific integral points available on $E_N$. Note the contrast with Section~4: the criterion is perfectly uniform in $N\bmod\ell$, while the number of solutions is markedly non-uniform in $\ell$. Uniformity in $N$ yields the barrier; non-uniformity in $\ell$ yields the reciprocity dichotomy.
\end{discussion}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[layer-4 denominator primes]
Input: $N$, an integral point $(x,y)$ on $E_N$, a bound $L$. Output: all primes $5\le\ell\le L$ with $\ell\nmid N$ dividing $\den x(4P)$. Compute $\Psi_4(N,x)$ once and test divisibility for each candidate prime. Cost: one evaluation of a degree-$9$ integer polynomial plus $O(\pi(L))$ divisions, rather than an exact rational quadrupling and the factorisation of a far larger denominator. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:crit}.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[layer totals]
Input: a prime $\ell\ge5$. Output: $T_4(\ell)$ and its predicted value. Brute force over the $\ell^2$ pairs $(c,t)$ costs $O(\ell^2)$ field operations; a refined $O(\ell)$ version counts fibres, adding $1+\chi(3)$ for each $t\ne0$ where $\chi$ is the quadratic character, and $1$ for $t=0$. Compare with $3\ell-2$ or $\ell$ according to Euler's criterion for $3$, or the class of $\ell$ modulo $12$. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:total}.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[barrier witness]
Input: $B$ and a semiprime $N=pq$ with $p,q>B$. Output: a prime $M>N$ with $M\equiv N\pmod{B!}$. Compute $B!$ and search $M=N+kB!$ for $k=1,2,\dots$, testing primality; termination is guaranteed by Dirichlet's theorem. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier}.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{Discussion and open problems}

\subsection{Summary}
At the quadrupling layer of the Mordell family: (i) for $\ell\ge5$, $\ell\nmid N$, one has $\ell\mid\den x(4P)\iff\ell\mid(x^3+N)(x^6+20Nx^3-8N^2)$, with Lemma~\ref{lem:key} as the structural source; (ii) the non-cancellation constants are the $\{2,3\}$-units $3^8$ and $2^63^2$; (iii) the layer total is $3\ell-2$ or $\ell$ according to the quadratic character of $3$, and no linear law with bounded error exists; (iv) layer $4$ is active exactly where layer $2$ is; (v) layers $2$--$4$ below any bound are blind to the factorisation of a semiprime.

\subsection{Open problems}

\textbf{(E1) A Chebotarev law for layer totals.} For every $n\ge2$, is there a finite Galois extension $K_n/\QQ(N)$ and a class function $f_n$ on its Galois group with $T_n(\ell)=f_n(\Frob_\ell)\cdot\ell+O_n(1)$ for all $\ell\ge5$ with $\ell\nmid n$, non-constant as soon as $\psi_n$ acquires a factor which is not linear in $N$? Known: $f_2\equiv1$, $f_3\equiv2$, $f_4\in\{1,3\}$ via $\QQ(\sqrt3)$. The first test is layer $5$, where $\psi_5$ has degree $12$ in $x$.

\textbf{(E2) Blindness along the $2$-power tower.} Layers $2$ and $4$ share their active residues. Is the cubic character of $-N$ the sole obstruction at every layer $2^k$, with the free-root mechanism confined to layers divisible by $3$?

\textbf{(E3) Valuations, not just occurrence.} For $N=55$ the valuation profile jumps from $7^2$ at layer $2$ to $7^2\cdot827^2\cdot1583^2$ at layer $4$. The formal group at $\ell$ predicts $v_\ell(\den x(2^kP))=v_\ell(\den x(2P))+2(k-1)$ for odd $\ell$ and $k\ge1$; proving this exact progression is $\ell$-adic bookkeeping on top of the criteria above and would sharpen the barrier discussion, valuations being the natural next statistic.

\textbf{(E4) Realisability.} The counting statements concern residue classes; upgrading them to statements about actual denominators requires integral points in the relevant classes. Conjecturally, for every prime $\ell\ge5$ and every residue $c$ there are infinitely many $N\equiv c\pmod\ell$ with $E_N(\ZZ)$ containing a point in every active class. The families $N=\ell^2-1$, $P=(1,\ell)$ and $N=1-\ell^3$, $P=(\ell,1)$ realise prescribed classes; the general question concerns representing residues by the form $y^2-x^3$.

\textbf{(E5) A quantitative ``no factor oracle'' theorem.} Fix a semiprime $N=pq$ and an integral point $P$ of infinite order. Heuristically $p$ divides $\den x(nP)$ only when $\bar P$ is trivial in $E_N(\FF_p)$, of probability $\approx1/\#E_N(\FF_p)\approx1/p$, whereas each good prime $\ell$ contributes at rate $\approx1/\ell$ and there are $\gg T$ of them below the height of $nP$ for $n\le T$. The expected number of occurrences of $p$ or $q$ for $n\le T$ should then be $O(\log\log T)$ against $\gg T$ in total. Making this a theorem requires height bounds along the orbit.

\subsection{Broader remarks}
Two features seem likely to generalise. First, reducing a torsion criterion to a fibre-counting problem after transposition (fixing $x$ and varying the parameter) turns division-polynomial combinatorics into character sums, and character sums into Frobenius data; the Kummer case is the degenerate one in which every fibre is a singleton. Second, the presence or absence of a parameter-free root of $\psi_n$ decides whether a layer can see all residues, a purely geometric condition on the universal curve. Finally, the coexistence of a criterion perfectly uniform in the parameter with a solution count wildly non-uniform in $\ell$ illustrates compactly why local data can be simultaneously very rich statistically and completely uninformative about global factorisation.

